  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatPre_4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "You\u2019ve heard about clinical trials, but up until recently, you
    just assumed that the most promising treatments identified during the discovery
    stage would move forward to be tested in people sooner. You wonder, what\u2019s
    the need for all these lab and animal studies if researchers already believe
    the new treatment could work?\r\n\r\nWhy does preclinical research matter for
    patients?\r"
  explanation: Preclinical research is an essential step before clinical trials can
    begin. Lab studies (in vitro) and animal models (in vivo) allow researchers to
    understand whether a new cancer treatment is safe enough for people and to identify
    potential risks. These studies take time, but they are critical for protecting
    patients and ensuring that only the safest and most promising treatments move
    forward into clinical trials.
  correctAnswer: Because it helps determine if a treatment is safe enough to test
    in people
  incorrectAnswer1: Because researchers are actively recruiting participants during
    this stage
  incorrectAnswer2: Because it helps determine the appropriate dosage of a new drug
    before it hits the market
  incorrectAnswer3: Because it tells us how well a new treatment works in humans
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Preclinical
